  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nLittle will change over the weekend and likely even for the bulk\nof next week. The high pressure ridge is heavily favored to remain\nover or at least very close to our region well into next week\nkeeping us under dry air aloft but with a persistent easterly low\nlevel semi-moist flow. Subsidence under the ridge and the dry air\nshould keep a lid on any potential convective chances most days.\nThere are some hints at maybe some convective potential early next\nweek over the highest peaks in eastern Arizona, but as of now the\nhighest PoPs barely breach 10% over the White Mountains.\n\nTemperatures are forecast to rise a bit further during the first\nhalf of next week with the western deserts likely reaching 100-104\ndegrees, while the south-central Arizona lower deserts may get\nclose to 100 degrees. These early October temperatures would range\nfrom 4-8 degrees above normal. Overnight lows will also be at\nleast a few degrees above normal, but generally staying in the\nupper 60s to lower 70s. The most interesting part of the forecast\nwill be the daily easterly breezy conditions due to the expected\nposition of the high over or just to our north and a weak trough\nsetting up to our south. The easterly winds should reach their\npeak over the weekend and during the mid to late morning hours \nwith gusts in some places reaching 30-35 mph. As daytime heating \nincreases and the mixed layer rises, the winds will be more \ndispersed and decrease during the afternoon hours. We should keep \nthese daily easterly mostly morning breezes through at early next\nweek and possibly longer.",
